-- Anki-AUTO-6047
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: YAML Lists</div><p>In YAML (YAML Ain\'t Markup Language), a list is written with a dash followed by a space before each item. Indentation then shows the nesting of the list.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>-</strong>: In YAML, a dash (-) at the start of a line indicates a list item.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>*</strong>: * is the YAML alias indicator (referring to an anchor), not a list marker.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>#</strong>: # starts a comment in YAML, not a list item.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>&gt;</strong>: &gt; starts a folded scalar (multi-line text), not a list.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>YAML list = dash and a space. Remember the pair:</p><ul><li>- is a list.</li><li>: is a key-value separator.</li><li># is a comment.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Automation and Programmability (CCNA / Day 60 Flashcards - JSON, XML, &amp; YAML)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-AUTO-6047';

-- Anki-AUTO-6062
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HTTP Response Classes - 1xx</div><p>HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) response status codes are grouped into five classes by their first digit: 1xx informational, 2xx successful, 3xx redirection, 4xx client error, 5xx server error.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>1xx</strong>: The 1xx class is the informational class. The server is saying &#x27;request received, continuing&#x27;.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>2xx</strong>: 2xx is the successful class, not informational.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>3xx</strong>: 3xx is the redirection class, not informational.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>4xx</strong>: 4xx is the client error class, not informational.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>1xx informational.</li><li>2xx successful.</li><li>3xx redirection.</li><li>4xx client error.</li><li>5xx server error.</li></ul><p>Learn the five first digits in order.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Automation and Programmability (CCNA / Day 61 Flashcards - REST APIs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-AUTO-6062';

-- Anki-AUTO-6085
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HTTP 500 Internal Server Error</div><p>500 means the server hit an unexpected condition and could not complete the request. The fault is entirely server-side.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>Internal Server Error</strong>: 500 means the server hit an unexpected condition and could not complete the request. The fault is entirely server-side.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Service Unavailable</strong>: Service Unavailable is 503. The server is temporarily unable to handle the request.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Not Implemented</strong>: Not Implemented is 501. The server does not support the requested functionality.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Bad Gateway</strong>: Bad Gateway is 502. The gateway got a bad response from an upstream server.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Know the 5xx family:</p><ul><li>500 generic failure.</li><li>501 not implemented.</li><li>502 bad gateway.</li><li>503 unavailable.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Automation and Programmability (CCNA / Day 61 Flashcards - REST APIs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-AUTO-6085';

-- Anki-AUTO-6087
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: SDN Architecture Layers</div><p>The SDN (Software-Defined Networking) architecture has three layers. The Application layer is the top layer: it holds the applications and scripts that request network services through the controller&#x27;s northbound interface.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>Application</strong>: The Application layer is the top layer of the SDN architecture: it holds the applications and scripts that request network services through the controller&#x27;s northbound interface.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Management</strong>: Management is a plane, not one of the three SDN architecture layers.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Infrastructure</strong>: Infrastructure is the bottom layer. The physical and virtual network devices.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Control</strong>: Control is the middle layer. It holds the SDN controller itself.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Top to bottom:</p><ul><li>Application (apps).</li><li>Control (controller).</li><li>Infrastructure (devices).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Automation and Programmability (CCNA / Day 62 Flashcards - SDN)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-AUTO-6087';

-- Anki-AUTO-6100
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: SD-Access Fabric Nodes</div><p>A SD-Access fabric has three node roles: Edge nodes connect endpoints, Border nodes connect the fabric to the outside, and Control nodes hold the LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol) mapping database.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>-Edge node -Border node -Control node</strong>: A SD-Access fabric has three node roles: Edge nodes connect endpoints, Border nodes connect the fabric to the outside, and Control nodes hold the LISP mapping database.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>-Edge node -Border node -Access node</strong>: Access node is not an SD-Access fabric role. The third role is the Control node.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>-Core node -Distribution node -Access node</strong>: Core, Distribution and Access are the classic three-tier campus layers, not SD-Access fabric roles.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>-Edge node -Control node -Policy node</strong>: Policy node is not an SD-Access role. The Border node is missing and there is no Policy node.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>SD-Access nodes:</p><ul><li>Edge (endpoints in).</li><li>Border (traffic out).</li><li>Control (LISP map).</li></ul><p>E-B-C.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Automation and Programmability (CCNA / Day 62 Flashcards - SDN)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-AUTO-6100';

-- Anki-AUTO-6103
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: SD-Access Control Plane</div><p>LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol) provides the control plane of SD-Access: the Control node uses the LISP mapping system to track which endpoint is behind which Edge node.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>LISP</strong>: LISP provides the control plane of SD-Access: the Control node uses the LISP mapping system to track which endpoint is behind which Edge node.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>VXLAN</strong>: VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) provides the data plane. It carries the traffic, it does not decide where it goes.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>BGP</strong>: BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) is a routing protocol used in the underlay, not the SD-Access control plane.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Cisco TrustSec</strong>: Cisco TrustSec provides policy control, not the control plane.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Control plane = LISP (where is it?).</li><li>data plane = VXLAN (carry it).</li><li>policy = TrustSec (is it allowed?).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Automation and Programmability (CCNA / Day 62 Flashcards - SDN)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-AUTO-6103';

-- Anki-IPC-3019
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: RIP</div><p>RIP (Routing Information Protocol) is the oldest and simplest dynamic routing protocol. It is a Distance Vector protocol that uses hop count as its metric, with a maximum of 15 hops.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>Routing Information Protocol</strong>: RIP stands for Routing Information Protocol.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Routing Internet Protocol</strong>: \'Internet\' is not in the name; RIP carries routing information, and the I stands for Information.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Remote Interface Protocol</strong>: \'Remote Interface Protocol\' is not a real protocol name.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Routed Information Protocol</strong>: \'Routed\' is not the word; the protocol is the Routing Information Protocol.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>RIP = Routing Information Protocol.</p><ul><li>Hop count metric.</li><li>max 15 hops.</li><li>AD (Administrative Distance) 120.</li><li>updates every 30 seconds.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 24 Flashcards - Dynamic Routing)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3019';

-- Anki-IPC-3039
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Administrative Distance Table</div><p>A directly connected network is the most trusted route source of all, because the router is physically attached to the network. Its Administrative Distance is 0.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>0</strong>: A directly connected route has an AD (Administrative Distance) of 0, the lowest (most trusted) value possible.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>1</strong>: 1 is the AD of a static route, not a directly connected route.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>20</strong>: 20 is the AD of eBGP, not a directly connected route.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>90</strong>: 90 is the AD of EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol), not a directly connected route.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>AD table to memorise:</p><ul><li>Connected 0.</li><li>Static 1.</li><li>eBGP 20.</li><li>EIGRP 90.</li><li>IGRP 100.</li><li>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) 110.</li><li>IS-IS (Intermediate System to Intermediate System) 115.</li><li>RIP (Routing Information Protocol) 120.</li><li>EIGRP ext 170.</li><li>iBGP 200.</li><li>unusable 255.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 24 Flashcards - Dynamic Routing)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3039';

-- Anki-IPC-3132
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Neighbor States</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) neighbors move through seven states in order: Down, Init, 2-way, Exstart, Exchange, Loading, Full. The first three are about discovering each other; the last four are about syncing the link-state databases.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>Down Init 2-way</strong>: The first three OSPF neighbor states are Down, Init and 2-way.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Init Exstart Exchange</strong>: That skips Down and 2-way, which come before Exstart.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Down 2-way Exstart</strong>: That skips Init, the state entered when a Hello is received.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Init 2-way Exstart</strong>: That starts at the second state and misses Down, the state before any Hello is received.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Neighbor states in order:</p><ul><li>Down.</li><li>Init.</li><li>2-way.</li><li>Exstart.</li><li>Exchange.</li><li>Loading.</li><li>Full.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3132';

-- Anki-IPC-3133
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Neighbor States</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) neighbors move through seven states in order: Down, Init, 2-way, Exstart, Exchange, Loading, Full. Init means a Hello was received but the neighbor has not seen us yet; 2-way means both sides have seen each other.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>Init 2-way Exstart</strong>: States 2, 3 and 4 are Init, 2-way and Exstart.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>2-way Exstart Exchange</strong>: That is states 3, 4 and 5, not 2, 3 and 4.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Down Init 2-way</strong>: That is states 1, 2 and 3, not 2, 3 and 4.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Init Exstart Exchange</strong>: That skips 2-way, which sits between Init and Exstart.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Neighbor states in order:</p><ul><li>Down.</li><li>Init.</li><li>2-way.</li><li>Exstart.</li><li>Exchange.</li><li>Loading.</li><li>Full.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3133';

-- Anki-IPC-3134
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Neighbor States</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) neighbors move through seven states in order: Down, Init, 2-way, Exstart, Exchange, Loading, Full. The DR (Designated Router)/BDR (Backup Designated Router) election happens in 2-way, then Exstart picks the master and slave for database exchange.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>2-way Exstart Exchange</strong>: States 3, 4 and 5 are 2-way, Exstart and Exchange.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Exstart Exchange Loading</strong>: That is states 4, 5 and 6, not 3, 4 and 5.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Init 2-way Exstart</strong>: That is states 2, 3 and 4, not 3, 4 and 5.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Down Init 2-way</strong>: That is states 1, 2 and 3, not 3, 4 and 5.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Neighbor states in order:</p><ul><li>Down.</li><li>Init.</li><li>2-way.</li><li>Exstart.</li><li>Exchange.</li><li>Loading.</li><li>Full.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3134';

-- Anki-IPC-3135
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Neighbor States</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) neighbors move through seven states in order: Down, Init, 2-way, Exstart, Exchange, Loading, Full. Exstart picks the master/slave, Exchange swaps DBD summaries, and Loading requests the missing LSAs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>Exstart Exchange Loading</strong>: States 4, 5 and 6 are Exstart, Exchange and Loading.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Init 2-way Exstart</strong>: That is states 2, 3 and 4, not 4, 5 and 6.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>2-way Exstart Exchange</strong>: That is states 3, 4 and 5, not 4, 5 and 6.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Exchange Loading Full</strong>: That is states 5, 6 and 7, not 4, 5 and 6.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Neighbor states in order:</p><ul><li>Down.</li><li>Init.</li><li>2-way.</li><li>Exstart.</li><li>Exchange.</li><li>Loading.</li><li>Full.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3135';

-- Anki-IPC-3136
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Neighbor States</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) neighbors move through seven states in order: Down, Init, 2-way, Exstart, Exchange, Loading, Full. In Exchange the routers swap DBDs, in Loading they request missing LSAs, and in Full they hold identical LSDBs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>Exchange Loading Full</strong>: States 5, 6 and 7 are Exchange, Loading and Full.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Exstart Exchange Loading</strong>: That is states 4, 5 and 6, not 5, 6 and 7.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Loading Full Down</strong>: Down is the first state, not a state after Full.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Exchange Full Init</strong>: Init comes second in the sequence, not after Full.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Neighbor states in order:</p><ul><li>Down.</li><li>Init.</li><li>2-way.</li><li>Exstart.</li><li>Exchange.</li><li>Loading.</li><li>Full.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3136';

-- Anki-IPC-3152
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>1</strong>: OSPF message type 1 is Hello.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>2</strong>: Type 2 is the Database Description (DBD) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>3</strong>: Type 3 is the Link State Request (LSR) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>5</strong>: Type 5 is the Link State Acknowledgement (LSAck) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3152';

-- Anki-IPC-3153
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>Hello</strong>: OSPF message type 1 is the Hello packet, used for neighbor discovery and keepalives.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Link-State Request (LSR)</strong>: That is message type 3, used to request missing LSAs.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Link-State Update (LSU)</strong>: That is message type 4, used to carry LSAs.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Database Description (DBD)</strong>: That is message type 2, used to summarize the LSDB (Link State Database).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3153';

-- Anki-IPC-3154
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>2</strong>: OSPF message type 2 is the Database Description (DBD) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>3</strong>: Type 3 is the Link State Request (LSR) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>1</strong>: Type 1 is the Hello packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>4</strong>: Type 4 is the Link State Update (LSU) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3154';

-- Anki-IPC-3155
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5). The DBD carries a list of LSA (Link State Advertisement) headers from the router\'s LSDB (Link State Database).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>Database Description (DBD)</strong>: OSPF message type 2 is the Database Description (DBD) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Link-State Request (LSR)</strong>: That is message type 3, used to request missing LSAs.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Link-State Update (LSU)</strong>: That is message type 4, used to carry LSAs.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Hello</strong>: That is message type 1, used for neighbor discovery.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3155';

-- Anki-IPC-3156
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>3</strong>: OSPF message type 3 is the Link State Request (LSR) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>5</strong>: Type 5 is the Link State Acknowledgement (LSAck) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>2</strong>: Type 2 is the Database Description (DBD) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>4</strong>: Type 4 is the Link State Update (LSU) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3156';

-- Anki-IPC-3157
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5). The LSR is sent in the Loading state to fetch missing LSAs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>Link-State Request (LSR)</strong>: OSPF message type 3 is the Link-State Request (LSR) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Hello</strong>: That is message type 1, used for neighbor discovery.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Database Description (DBD)</strong>: That is message type 2, used to summarize the LSDB (Link State Database).</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Link-State Update (LSU)</strong>: That is message type 4, used to carry LSAs.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3157';

-- Anki-IPC-3158
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>4</strong>: OSPF message type 4 is the Link State Update (LSU) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>2</strong>: Type 2 is the Database Description (DBD) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>5</strong>: Type 5 is the Link State Acknowledgement (LSAck) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>3</strong>: Type 3 is the Link State Request (LSR) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3158';

-- Anki-IPC-3159
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5). The LSU is the packet that actually carries LSAs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>Link-State Update (LSU)</strong>: OSPF message type 4 is the Link-State Update (LSU) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Database Description (DBD)</strong>: That is message type 2, used to summarize the LSDB (Link State Database).</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Hello</strong>: That is message type 1, used for neighbor discovery.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Link-State Request (LSR)</strong>: That is message type 3, used to request missing LSAs.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3159';

-- Anki-IPC-3160
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>5</strong>: OSPF message type 5 is the Link State Acknowledgement (LSAck) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>3</strong>: Type 3 is the Link State Request (LSR) packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>1</strong>: Type 1 is the Hello packet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>4</strong>: Type 4 is the Link State Update (LSU) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3160';

-- Anki-IPC-3161
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Packet Types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) defines five packet types, numbered 1 to 5: Hello (1), Database Description (2), Link State Request (3), Link State Update (4) and Link State Acknowledgement (5). The LSAck confirms receipt of an LSU.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>Link-State Acknowledgement (LSAck)</strong>: OSPF message type 5 is the Link-State Acknowledgement (LSAck) packet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Link-State Request (LSR)</strong>: That is message type 3, used to request missing LSAs.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Link-State Update (LSU)</strong>: That is message type 4, used to carry LSAs.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Hello</strong>: That is message type 1, used for neighbor discovery.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Packet types in order:</p><ul><li>1 Hello.</li><li>2 DBD.</li><li>3 LSR.</li><li>4 LSU.</li><li>5 LSAck.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 27 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3161';

-- Anki-IPC-3205
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF neighbour requirements</div><p>For two routers to become OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) neighbours, several parameters must match: area ID, subnet, Hello and Dead timers, authentication, and (for the adjacency to reach Full) MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit) and network type.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>Yes</strong>: Yes. The OSPF Hello and Dead timers must match on both routers for them to become neighbours.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>No</strong>: If the timers differ, the routers will not become neighbours at all.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Only the Hello timer has to match</strong>: Both timers must match, not only Hello. A Dead mismatch also blocks the adjacency.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Only the Dead timer has to match</strong>: Both timers must match, not only Dead. A Hello mismatch also blocks the adjacency.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Neighbour checklist for the exam:</p><ul><li>same area.</li><li>same subnet.</li><li>same Hello/Dead timers.</li><li>same authentication.</li><li>same MTU.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 28 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3205';

-- Anki-IPC-3210
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: OSPF LSA types</div><p>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) routers describe the topology with Link State Advertisements (LSAs). Type 1 is the Router LSA (Link State Advertisement), generated by every router to describe its own links; Type 2 is the Network LSA, Type 3 is the Summary LSA, Type 5 is the AS-External LSA.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>1</strong>: OSPF Type 1 LSA is the Router LSA. So the blank is 1.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>2</strong>: Type 2 is the Network LSA, generated by the DR (Designated Router) to describe the multi-access network.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>3</strong>: Type 3 is the Summary LSA, generated by the ABR (Area Border Router) to advertise networks between areas.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>5</strong>: Type 5 is the AS-External LSA, generated by the ASBR (Autonomous System Boundary Router) to advertise external routes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Learn the LSA numbers in order:</p><ul><li>1 Router.</li><li>2 Network.</li><li>3 Summary.</li><li>4 ASBR Summary.</li><li>5 AS-External.</li><li>7 NSSA External.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 28 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3210';

-- Anki-IPC-3227
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HSRP proprietary status</div><p>HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) was created by Cisco and is Cisco proprietary. It only runs on Cisco devices. VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) is the open-standard alternative.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>Yes</strong>: Yes. HSRP is Cisco proprietary.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>No</strong>: HSRP is not an open standard; VRRP is the open-standard FHRP (First Hop Redundancy Protocol).</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Yes, but only version 1</strong>: Both HSRPv1 and HSRPv2 are Cisco proprietary, not just version 1.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Yes, but only version 2</strong>: Both HSRPv1 and HSRPv2 are Cisco proprietary, not just version 2.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Exam shortcut:</p><ul><li>HSRP = Cisco.</li><li>VRRP = open standard.</li><li>GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) = Cisco.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3227';

-- Anki-IPC-3229
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: GLBP proprietary status</div><p>GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) was created by Cisco and is Cisco proprietary. It only runs on Cisco devices.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>Yes</strong>: Yes. GLBP is Cisco proprietary.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>No</strong>: GLBP is not an open standard; it is Cisco proprietary.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Yes, but only version 1</strong>: GLBP has no open-standard version; the protocol itself is Cisco proprietary.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Only on Catalyst 6500 switches</strong>: GLBP is not limited to one switch model; it is Cisco proprietary across Cisco platforms.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Exam shortcut:</p><ul><li>HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) = Cisco.</li><li>VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) = open standard.</li><li>GLBP = Cisco.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3229';

-- Anki-IPC-3230
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HSRP roles</div><p>HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) has two roles: the Active router forwards traffic for the virtual IP (Internet Protocol), and the Standby router waits to take over if the Active fails.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>Active / standby</strong>: HSRP uses Active and standby routers.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Primary / Secondary</strong>: Primary / Secondary are not FHRP (First Hop Redundancy Protocol) role names in the CCNA syllabus.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Master / Backup</strong>: Master / Backup are the VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) role names, not HSRP.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>AVG / AVF</strong>: AVG / AVF (Active Virtual Gateway / Active Virtual Forwarder) are the GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) role names, not HSRP.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Active/Standby = HSRP.</li><li>Master/Backup = VRRP.</li><li>AVG/AVF = GLBP.</li></ul><p>Learn the pairs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3230';

-- Anki-IPC-3231
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: VRRP roles</div><p>VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) has two roles: the Master router forwards traffic for the virtual IP (Internet Protocol), and the Backup routers wait to take over if the Master fails.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>Master / Backup</strong>: VRRP uses Master and Backup routers.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>Active / Standby</strong>: Active / Standby are the HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) role names, not VRRP.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Primary / Secondary</strong>: Primary / Secondary are not FHRP (First Hop Redundancy Protocol) role names in the CCNA syllabus.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>AVG / AVF</strong>: AVG / AVF (Active Virtual Gateway / Active Virtual Forwarder) are the GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) role names, not VRRP.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Active/Standby = HSRP.</li><li>Master/Backup = VRRP.</li><li>AVG/AVF = GLBP.</li></ul><p>Learn the pairs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3231';

-- Anki-IPC-3232
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HSRP multicast addresses</div><p>HSRPv1 routers exchange Hello messages on the multicast address 224.0.0.2 (all routers on the segment). HSRPv2 uses a different address, 224.0.0.102.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>224.0.0.2</strong>: HSRPv1 uses the multicast IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4) address 224.0.0.2.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>224.0.0.10</strong>: 224.0.0.10 is the EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol) multicast address, not HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol).</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>224.0.0.102</strong>: 224.0.0.102 is the HSRPv2 (and GLBP) multicast address, not HSRPv1.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>224.0.0.18</strong>: 224.0.0.18 is the VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) multicast address, not HSRP.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>HSRPv1 = .2.</li><li>HSRPv2 = .102.</li><li>VRRP = .18.</li><li>GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) = .102.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3232';

-- Anki-IPC-3236
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HSRP virtual MAC addresses</div><p>Each FHRP (First Hop Redundancy Protocol) has its own virtual MAC (Media Access Control) format. HSRPv1 uses 0000.0c07.acXX, where XX is the HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) group number in hex. The 0c07.ac prefix is Cisco\'s HSRP identifier.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>0000.0c07.ac XX ( XX = HSRP group number)</strong>: HSRPv1 virtual MAC address format is 0000.0c07.ac XX ( XX = HSRP group number).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>0007.b400. XXYY ( XX = GLBP group number, YY = AVF number)</strong>: 0007.b400. XXYY is the GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) virtual MAC format, not HSRP.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>0000.5e00.01 XX ( XX = VRRP group number)</strong>: 0000.5e00.01 XX is the VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) virtual MAC format, not HSRP.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>0000.0c9f.f XXX ( XXX = HSRP group number)</strong>: 0000.0c9f.f XXX is the HSRPv2 virtual MAC format, not v1.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Spot the protocol from the MAC:</p><ul><li>0c07.ac = HSRPv1.</li><li>0c9f.f = HSRPv2.</li><li>5e00.01 = VRRP.</li><li>b400 = GLBP.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3236';

-- Anki-IPC-3238
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: VRRP virtual MAC address</div><p>VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) uses the virtual MAC (Media Access Control) format 0000.5e00.01XX, where XX is the VRID (VRRP group number). The 0000.5e00.01 prefix is the IANA-assigned VRRP identifier.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>0000.5e00.01 XX ( XX = VRRP group number)</strong>: VRRP virtual MAC address format is 0000.5e00.01 XX ( XX = VRRP group number).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>0007.b400. XXYY ( XX = GLBP group number, YY = AVF number)</strong>: 0007.b400. XXYY is the GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) virtual MAC format, not VRRP.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>0000.0c07.ac XX ( XX = HSRP group number)</strong>: 0000.0c07.ac XX is the HSRPv1 virtual MAC format, not VRRP.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>0000.0c9f.f XXX ( XXX = HSRP group number)</strong>: 0000.0c9f.f XXX is the HSRPv2 virtual MAC format, not VRRP.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Spot the protocol from the MAC:</p><ul><li>5e00.01 = VRRP.</li><li>0c07.ac = HSRPv1.</li><li>0c9f.f = HSRPv2.</li><li>b400 = GLBP.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3238';

-- Anki-IPC-3248
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HSRP virtual IP configuration</div><p>The HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) virtual IP (Internet Protocol) is configured on the interface with the standby command, giving the group number and the virtual IP, for example standby 1 ip 192.168.1.1.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>standby group-number ip ip-address</strong>: The command to configure the HSRP virtual IP on an interface is standby group-number ip ip-address.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>hsrp group-number ip ip-address</strong>: hsrp group-number ip ip-address is not a valid IOS command; the command starts with standby.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>standby ip ip-address</strong>: standby ip ip-address is missing the group number; the full form needs the group first.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>standby group-number ip-address</strong>: standby group-number ip-address is missing the ip keyword before the address.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>HSRP interface commands all start with standby.</p><ul><li>Standby.</li><li>priority.</li><li>preempt.</li><li>version.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3248';

-- Anki-IPC-3251
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: HSRP version</div><p>HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) version 2 is enabled on an interface with the standby version 2 command. Version 2 supports more groups (0-4095), a different multicast address (224.0.0.102) and a different virtual MAC (Media Access Control) format.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>standby version 2</strong>: The command to configure HSRP version 2 is standby version 2.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>standby version v2</strong>: The version is given as the number 2, not v2; standby version v2 is not valid.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>standby group-number version 2</strong>: The version command does not take a group number; standby group-number version 2 is not valid.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>hsrp version 2</strong>: hsrp version 2 is not a valid IOS command; the command starts with standby.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>HSRPv2 upgrades:</p><ul><li>groups up to 4095.</li><li>multicast .102.</li><li>and the 0000.0c9f.f MAC format.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 29 Flashcards - FHRPs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3251';

-- Anki-IPC-3257
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Viewing the routing table</div><p>The show ip route command displays the routing table, with a code letter for each route source (C connected, L local, S static, O OSPF, D EIGRP, R RIP) and the administrative distance and metric.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>show ip route</strong>: The command to show the routing table is show ip route.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>show ip route table</strong>: show ip route table is not a valid IOS command; the correct form is show ip route.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>show routes</strong>: show routes is not a valid IOS command.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>show ip routing table</strong>: show ip routing table is not a valid IOS command; the correct form is show ip route.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Read show ip route like a table:</p><ul><li>code.</li><li>network.</li><li>AD (Administrative Distance)/metric.</li><li>next hop.</li><li>exit interface.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 11 (part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3257';

-- Anki-IPC-3258
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Routing table codes</div><p>In the output of show ip route, each route has a code letter. C stands for Connected. A network directly attached to one of the router\'s interfaces.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>Connected</strong>: Code C in the routing table means Connected.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Candidate default</strong>: Candidate default is shown with an asterisk (*), not with the letter C.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>CEF</strong>: CEF is Cisco Express Forwarding, the forwarding mechanism. Not a routing table code.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>Configured</strong>: Configured is not a routing table code; the code for a connected network is C.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>The common codes:</p><ul><li>C Connected.</li><li>L Local.</li><li>S Static.</li><li>O OSPF (Open Shortest Path First).</li><li>D EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol).</li><li>R RIP (Routing Information Protocol).</li><li>* candidate default.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 11 (part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3258';

-- Anki-IPC-3275
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Routing table codes</div><p>In the output of show ip route, each route has a code letter for its source. S stands for Static. A route configured manually with the ip route command.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>S</strong>: Code S in the routing table indicates a static route.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>R</strong>: R stands for RIP (Routing Information Protocol), a dynamically learned route. Not a static route.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>O</strong>: O stands for OSPF (Open Shortest Path First), a dynamically learned route. Not a static route.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>D</strong>: D stands for EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol), a dynamically learned route. Not a static route.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>S = Static. The common codes:</p><ul><li>C Connected.</li><li>L Local.</li><li>S Static.</li><li>O OSPF.</li><li>D EIGRP.</li><li>R RIP.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 11 (part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3275';

-- Anki-IPS-4031
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: IP Protocol Numbers</div><p>The IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4) header has a Protocol field that identifies the transport protocol carried inside the packet. ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol) is assigned protocol number 1.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option C</span> <strong>ICMP</strong>: ICMP is IP (Internet Protocol) protocol number 1 in the IPv4 header\'s Protocol field.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>UDP</strong>: UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is protocol number 17</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>TCP</strong>: TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is protocol number 6</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>IGMP</strong>: IGMP (Internet Group Management Protocol) is protocol number 2</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the set:</p><ul><li>ICMP 1.</li><li>IGMP 2.</li><li>TCP 6.</li><li>UDP 17.</li><li>EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol) 88.</li><li>OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) 89.</li></ul><p>The exam loves them.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 35 Flashcards - Extended ACLs)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4031';

-- Anki-IPS-4065
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: NTP Port</div><p>NTP (Network Time Protocol) uses UDP (User Datagram Protocol) port 123. It runs over UDP because time synchronisation is a small, fast exchange that tolerates the occasional lost packet.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>NTP</strong>: NTP uses UDP port 123.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>SNMP</strong>: SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) uses UDP ports 161/162</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>Syslog</strong>: Syslog uses UDP port 514</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>DNS</strong>: DNS (Domain Name System) uses UDP/TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) port 53</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>NTP 123.</li><li>DNS 53.</li><li>SNMP 161.</li><li>Syslog 514.</li></ul><p>Four ports, four protocols, the exam tests them as a set.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 37 Flashcards - NTP)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4065';

-- Anki-IPS-4082
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: NTP Authentication</div><p>NTP (Network Time Protocol) authentication has three steps: create a key (ntp authentication-key), mark it trusted (ntp trusted-key), and enable authentication (ntp authenticate).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option B</span> <strong>ntp authenticate</strong>: ntp authenticate turns on NTP authentication globally; keys are then configured separately.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>ntp trusted-key</strong>: that marks a key as trusted; it does not enable authentication</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>ntp authentication</strong>: not a valid command; the keyword is authenticate</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>ntp authentication-key</strong>: that creates a key; enabling authentication is a separate step</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Three steps:</p><ul><li>create the key (authentication-key).</li><li>trust it (trusted-key).</li><li>enable it (authenticate).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 37 Flashcards - NTP)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4082';

-- Anki-IPS-4083
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: NTP Authentication Keys</div><p>ntp authentication-key &lt;number&gt; md5 &lt;key&gt; creates an MD5 authentication key with the given number. The key must then be trusted and authentication enabled before it is used.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>ntp authentication-key key-number md5 key</strong>: ntp authentication-key &lt;number&gt; md5 &lt;key&gt; creates an MD5 authentication key with the given number.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>ntp trusted-key key-number md5 key</strong>: trusted-key marks an existing key as trusted; it does not create one</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>ntp authenticate key-number md5 key</strong>: authenticate enables authentication; it takes no key</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>ntp authentication-key key-number sha key</strong>: NTP (Network Time Protocol) authentication keys use MD5, not SHA</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>authentication-key creates.</li><li>trusted-key trusts.</li><li>authenticate enables.</li></ul><p>Three commands, three jobs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 37 Flashcards - NTP)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4083';

-- Anki-IPS-4167
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Syslog Message Format</div><p>A Syslog message has the format seq:timestamp: %facility-severity-mnemonic:description. Each field tells you something different about the event.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>seq</strong>: The first field in the format is seq: a number that indicates the order of messages.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>timestamp</strong>: timestamp is a different field: indicates the time the message was generated.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>facility</strong>: facility is a different field: indicates which process on the device generated the message.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>severity</strong>: severity is a different field: indicates how serious the logged event is.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Learn the format in order:</p><ul><li>seq.</li><li>timestamp.</li><li>facility.</li><li>severity.</li><li>mnemonic.</li><li>description.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 41 Flashcards - Syslog)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4167';

