-- Anki-IPS-4216
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Telnet port number</div><p>Telnet servers listen on the well-known TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) port 23. Port 22 belongs to SSH (Secure Shell), port 21 to FTP (File Transfer Protocol) control, and port 69 to TFTP (Trivial File Transfer Protocol).</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>23</strong>: 23: Telnet servers listen on TCP port 23.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>69</strong>: Port 69 is TFTP, not Telnet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>22</strong>: Port 22 is SSH, not Telnet.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>21</strong>: Port 21 is the FTP control connection, not Telnet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>22 = SSH.</li><li>23 = Telnet.</li><li>21 = FTP control.</li><li>20 = FTP data.</li><li>69 = TFTP.</li></ul><p>Learn them as a group.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 42 Flashcards - SSH)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4216';

-- Anki-IPS-4302
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: Dynamic NAT</div><p>In dynamic NAT (Network Address Translation) the router automatically creates one-to-one mappings between inside local and inside global addresses, choosing from a configured pool of public addresses as inside hosts send traffic.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>Dynamic NAT</strong>: Dynamic NAT uses a pool: when an inside host sends traffic, the router picks a free public address from the pool and creates the mapping automatically.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>NAT64</strong>: NAT64 translates between IPv6 (Internet Protocol version 6) and IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4); it is not a form of IPv4 address pooling.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>Static NAT</strong>: Static NAT mappings are one-to-one but are configured manually by the administrator, not created automatically.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>PAT / NAT Overload</strong>: PAT (Port Address Translation) maps many inside addresses to one public address using port numbers. Many-to-one, not one-to-one.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Static = manual one-to-one.</li><li>Dynamic = automatic one-to-one from a pool.</li><li>PAT = many-to-one with ports.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 45 Flashcards - NAT (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4302';

-- Anki-IPS-4317
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: PoE Standards - Type 1</div><p>PoE (Power over Ethernet) Type 1 is defined by IEEE 802.3af (2003), delivering up to 15W over 2 pairs.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option A</span> <strong>802.3af</strong>: 802.3af is the original PoE standard. Type 1.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>802.3az</strong>: 802.3az is Energy Efficient Ethernet, not a PoE standard.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>802.3bt</strong>: 802.3bt is UPoE (Universal Power over Ethernet)/UPoE+ (Types 3 and 4).</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option D</span> <strong>802.3at</strong>: 802.3at is PoE+ (Type 2, 30W).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>af = Type 1 (15W).</li><li>at = Type 2 (30W).</li><li>bt = Types 3/4 (60W/100W).</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 46 Flashcards - QoS (Part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4317';

-- Anki-IPS-4363
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: DSCP Values</div><p>DSCP (Differentiated Services Code Point) values run 0-63 in the 6-bit DSCP field. Class selectors are CSx = 8x, assured forwarding is AFxy = 8x + 2y, EF is 46 and DF is 0.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>DF</strong>: DF is the DSCP 0 marking.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>EF</strong>: EF is DSCP 46, not DSCP 0.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>CS1</strong>: CS1 is DSCP 8, not DSCP 0.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>AF11</strong>: AF11 is DSCP 10, not DSCP 0.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the formula:</p><ul><li>CSx = 8x.</li><li>AFxy = 8x + 2y.</li><li>EF = 46.</li><li>DF = 0.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📋 IPv4 Header - Full Reference (Memorize This):</div><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;font-size:13px;\"><tr><td colspan=\"4\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Version<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">4 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"4\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">IHL<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">4 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"6\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">DSCP<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">6 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">ECN<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">2 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"16\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Total Length<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">16 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"16\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Identification<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">16 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"3\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Flags<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">3 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"13\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Fragment Offset<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">13 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"8\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">TTL<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">8 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"8\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Protocol<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">8 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"16\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Header Checksum<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">16 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"32\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Source Address<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">32 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"32\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Destination Address<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">32 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"32\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Options + Padding<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">0-40 bytes</span></td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;margin-top:10px;\"><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Version</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(4 bits)</span>: IP version (4)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>IHL (Internet Header Length)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(4 bits)</span>: Header length in 32-bit words (5-15); IHL × 4 = header bytes (20-60)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>DSCP (Differentiated Services Code Point)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(6 bits)</span>: QoS marking (0-63)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>ECN (Explicit Congestion Notification)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(2 bits)</span>: Congestion notification (0-3)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Total Length</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(16 bits)</span>: Whole packet length in bytes, header + data (max 65,535)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Identification</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(16 bits)</span>: Same ID for all fragments of one packet</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Flags</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(3 bits)</span>: Reserved (0), DF (Don\'t Fragment), MF (More Fragments)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Fragment Offset</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(13 bits)</span>: Position of this fragment in the original packet (in 8-byte units)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>TTL (Time to Live)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(8 bits)</span>: Max hops; each router subtracts 1, drops the packet at 0</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Protocol</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(8 bits)</span>: Next protocol: 1 = ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol), 6 = TCP (Transmission Control Protocol), 17 = UDP (User Datagram Protocol), 89 = OSPF (Open Shortest Path First)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Header Checksum</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(16 bits)</span>: Error check on the header only (recalculated at each hop)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Source Address</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(32 bits)</span>: Sender IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4) address</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Destination Address</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(32 bits)</span>: Receiver IPv4 address</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Options</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(0-40 bytes)</span>: Rarely used (e.g. Record Route, Timestamp); Padding pads the header to a multiple of 32 bits</div></div><p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">Total: 20 bytes minimum, up to 60 bytes with Options. IHL × 4 = header length in bytes.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 47 Flashcards - QoS (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4363';

-- Anki-IPS-4364
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class=\"explanation-rich-content\"><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Core Concept: DSCP Values - DF</div><p>DF (Default Forwarding) is the best-effort per-hop behaviour, marked DSCP (Differentiated Services Code Point) 0.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card correct-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-correct\">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag correct\">Option D</span> <strong>0</strong>: DF is DSCP 0.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card incorrect-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-incorrect\">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option A</span> <strong>10</strong>: DSCP 10 is the value for AF11, not for DF.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option B</span> <strong>46</strong>: DSCP 46 is the value for EF (expedited forwarding), not for DF.</li><li><span class=\"opt-tag wrong\">Option C</span> <strong>8</strong>: DSCP 8 is the value for CS1, not for DF.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card tip-card\"><h4 class=\"section-title-tip\">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the formula:</p><ul><li>CSx = 8x.</li><li>AFxy = 8x + 2y.</li><li>EF = 46.</li><li>DF = 0.</li></ul></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📋 IPv4 Header - Full Reference (Memorize This):</div><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;font-size:13px;\"><tr><td colspan=\"4\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Version<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">4 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"4\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">IHL<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">4 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"6\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">DSCP<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">6 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">ECN<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">2 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"16\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Total Length<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">16 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"16\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Identification<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">16 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"3\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Flags<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">3 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"13\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Fragment Offset<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">13 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"8\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">TTL<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">8 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"8\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Protocol<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">8 bits</span></td><td colspan=\"16\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Header Checksum<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">16 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"32\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Source Address<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">32 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"32\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Destination Address<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">32 bits</span></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"32\" style=\"border:1px solid #4a5568;padding:6px;text-align:center;\">Options + Padding<br><span style=\"font-size:12px;\">0-40 bytes</span></td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;margin-top:10px;\"><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Version</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(4 bits)</span>: IP version (4)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>IHL (Internet Header Length)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(4 bits)</span>: Header length in 32-bit words (5-15); IHL × 4 = header bytes (20-60)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>DSCP (Differentiated Services Code Point)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(6 bits)</span>: QoS marking (0-63)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>ECN (Explicit Congestion Notification)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(2 bits)</span>: Congestion notification (0-3)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Total Length</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(16 bits)</span>: Whole packet length in bytes, header + data (max 65,535)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Identification</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(16 bits)</span>: Same ID for all fragments of one packet</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Flags</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(3 bits)</span>: Reserved (0), DF (Don\'t Fragment), MF (More Fragments)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Fragment Offset</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(13 bits)</span>: Position of this fragment in the original packet (in 8-byte units)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>TTL (Time to Live)</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(8 bits)</span>: Max hops; each router subtracts 1, drops the packet at 0</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Protocol</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(8 bits)</span>: Next protocol: 1 = ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol), 6 = TCP (Transmission Control Protocol), 17 = UDP (User Datagram Protocol), 89 = OSPF (Open Shortest Path First)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Header Checksum</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(16 bits)</span>: Error check on the header only (recalculated at each hop)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Source Address</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(32 bits)</span>: Sender IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4) address</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Destination Address</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(32 bits)</span>: Receiver IPv4 address</div><div style=\"margin-bottom:7px;\"><strong>Options</strong> <span style=\"opacity:0.75;\">(0-40 bytes)</span>: Rarely used (e.g. Record Route, Timestamp); Padding pads the header to a multiple of 32 bits</div></div><p style=\"margin-top:8px;\">Total: 20 bytes minimum, up to 60 bytes with Options. IHL × 4 = header length in bytes.</p></div><div class=\"expl-card concept-card\"><div class=\"section-title-concept\">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 47 Flashcards - QoS (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4364';

